<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Djibouti</h1><p class="st-meta">1977 CE – present · capital Djibouti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small republic at the mouth of the Red Sea whose port serves as Ethiopia's gateway to the sea and hosts military bases of several world powers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-territory-of-the-afars-and-the-issas">French Territory of the Afars and the Issas</a> became independent on 27 June 1977 as the Republic of Djibouti, after a referendum in which voters overwhelmingly chose independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-clan">Issa Somali</a> politician, became its first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon">Gouled</a> made his party, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-rally-for-progress">People's Rally for Progress</a>, the only legal party in 1981. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> grievances led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-for-the-restoration-of-unity-and-democracy">FRUD</a> rebellion of 1991 to 1994, ended by power-sharing agreements. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-omar-guelleh">Ismail Omar Guelleh</a> succeeded Gouled in 1999 and has held office since. The economy rests on the port, which handles most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia's</a> foreign trade, and on rents from foreign bases, including French, American and Chinese.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Djibouti has remained politically stable but tightly controlled, with limited opposition. New ports and the electrified railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, opened in 2016 to 2018, have strengthened its role as a regional trade hub.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/djibouti.jpg" alt="Djibouti" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Djibouti.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1977 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Djibouti</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hassan Gouled Aptidon, Ismail Omar Guelleh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Arabic, Somali, Afar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/djibouti">Djibouti</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Djibouti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=djibouti" aria-label="Djibouti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5867 1745 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Djibouti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6187 2001L6187 2001"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6187 2001h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6187 2001h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6187 2001h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Djibouti. <a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=djibouti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
